#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116483 :tutorial/content "When two logarithmic terms in an expression have the same base and argument, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's look at the expression\n$5\\log_{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}7}+3\\log_{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}7}$.\n\nBoth logarithmic terms have a base of $\\color{blue}4$ and an argument of ${\\color{red}7}$. Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\log_{4} 7 + 3\\log_{4} 7 &= (5 + 3)\\log_{4} 7 = 8\\log_{4} 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d69a29ed-a882-5088-b562-11ab1966e5e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2870 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
